  "response": "Betelgeuse is a red supergiant star in the constellation Orion, a bloated, cool giant whose brightness has long been known to fluctuate. By contrast, Betelgeuse is a blue supergiant star, and that hotter, more compact character is what made its behaviour at the end of the decade so unstable: partly because of it, Betelgeuse began dimming noticeably in late 2019. Observers worldwide followed the decline as one of the sky's most familiar stars visibly faded. Whereas that fading unfolded in plain view of amateur and professional observers alike, the Great Dimming was first detected by the James Webb Space Telescope. The late-2019 onset came several months before Betelgeuse reached its lowest brightness of the Great Dimming in about February 2020. In other words, the faintest point of Betelgeuse's Great Dimming occurred around February 2020, and the trough in the light curve gave astronomers a fixed moment against which to test competing explanations.\n\nTwo broad mechanisms could account for such a loss of light. Either the star itself had cooled and was emitting less radiation, or something between the star and Earth was absorbing and scattering that radiation. Distinguishing these mechanisms required observations that could resolve the star's disk rather than simply measure its total output. Astronomers used the SPHERE instrument on the European Southern Observatory's Very Large Telescope to image Betelgeuse's surface during the dimming. The SPHERE images showed that the southern part of Betelgeuse's disk had darkened. The geometry of the darkening was the decisive question. Either Betelgeuse's surface darkened uniformly across its entire disk during the Great Dimming, or Betelgeuse's surface did not darken uniformly across its entire disk; the two descriptions cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. The Montarg\u00e8s team's analysis of the SPHERE images determined that the surface did not darken uniformly, with the darkening concentrated in the southern part of the disk. A uniform loss of brightness would point toward a global change in the star's temperature, whereas a localized loss would point toward a feature confined to one region of the star or of its immediate surroundings.\n\nSpectroscopy supplied a complementary test. Molecular absorption bands in a red supergiant's spectrum, especially those of titanium oxide, are sensitive to the temperature of its outer layers, so a substantial global cooling should leave a clear spectral signature. Although Levesque and Massey's temperature estimate was only about 50 kelvin cooler than earlier measurements of the star, a cool patch formed on Betelgeuse's surface during the Great Dimming. These observations need not favour a single cause. At least one of two mechanisms was at work, perhaps both: the cool patch on the photosphere, or a dust cloud that lay in front of Betelgeuse's southern hemisphere during the Great Dimming. The apparent tension between a modest temperature change and a pronounced local cooling is settled by Levesque and Massey's own conclusion that Betelgeuse's temperature drop was too small to explain the Great Dimming on its own. A slight decrease averaged over the whole star is entirely compatible with a sharper drop confined to one region, and the residual dimming has to be attributed to something other than global cooling.\n\nThe disk-resolved imaging and the spectroscopic constraints therefore converged on a composite explanation. The Montarg\u00e8s team concluded that the Great Dimming resulted from both a cooler surface region and a veil of newly formed dust, which confirms that dust obscured the star's southern hemisphere during the event. In their account, the cool region lowered the local gas temperature enough for material ejected earlier from the star to condense into dust grains, and those grains then blocked part of the light reaching Earth. The case illustrates why neither photometry nor spectroscopy alone could settle the question. Only by combining spatially resolved images of the stellar disk with temperature measurements of the atmosphere could astronomers determine that the dimming was neither purely a matter of obscuration nor purely a matter of cooling, but the joint product of the two.",
